1
00:00:09,432 --> 00:00:11,541
All right, let's jump into IPsec.

2
00:00:11,541 --> 00:00:13,259
So we have the basics in place.

3
00:00:13,259 --> 00:00:16,713
Now let's see them being applied.

4
00:00:16,713 --> 00:00:19,922
So, getting back to why we need this.

5
00:00:19,922 --> 00:00:25,623
And so the example here is, a company
has two sites and they have employees at

6
00:00:25,623 --> 00:00:31,154
these different sites that want to
share information with each other.

7
00:00:31,154 --> 00:00:37,194
Now, if the attacker can see the traffic,
they'll get access to some secret data.

8
00:00:37,194 --> 00:00:41,750
And so we're going to talk about
IPsec as a solution to this.

9
00:00:43,400 --> 00:00:51,428
So stepping back, it'd be useful to even
talk about how would somebody eavesdrop.

10
00:00:51,428 --> 00:00:56,429
So we learned at the link layer,
WiFi is a broadcast medium,

11
00:00:56,429 --> 00:00:59,640
so it's broadcast communication.

12
00:00:59,640 --> 00:01:04,620
And so if you're in a public space,
you can see that broadcast traffic.

13
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:12,437
A network provider where your traffic goes
through could be compelled by a government

14
00:01:12,437 --> 00:01:18,508
agency to provide a backdoor for them,
or maybe they have a rogue employee.

15
00:01:18,508 --> 00:01:23,310
Or maybe there's
a compromised router that has

16
00:01:23,310 --> 00:01:28,240
a software bug in it,
security vulnerability.

17
00:01:28,240 --> 00:01:32,840
So it could be at
the network provider level.

18
00:01:32,840 --> 00:01:39,636
We've also talked about kind of BGP and
OSPF and routing protocols.

19
00:01:39,636 --> 00:01:46,480
And a simple misconfiguration
could actually cause problems.

20
00:01:46,480 --> 00:01:48,420
So it might not be intentional, but

21
00:01:48,420 --> 00:01:51,470
it might actually redirect
traffic to the wrong place.

22
00:01:52,480 --> 00:01:57,382
Or it could have been intentional
through route hijacking,

23
00:01:57,382 --> 00:02:00,420
which can redirect the traffic.

24
00:02:00,420 --> 00:02:04,340
It would be something that
the attacker would intentionally do.

25
00:02:04,340 --> 00:02:09,146
So all of these provide this
opportunity to eavesdrop,

26
00:02:09,146 --> 00:02:13,930
and there's no one solution
that solves everything.

27
00:02:13,930 --> 00:02:18,754
And that's why we're talking about
a bunch of different solutions that you

28
00:02:18,754 --> 00:02:23,140
might want to use in different
scenarios or use them in combination.

29
00:02:24,700 --> 00:02:31,716
So IPsec, what it does is it creates
an encrypted tunnel between two endpoints.

30
00:02:31,716 --> 00:02:36,549
It could be used for
a whole site as shown in this picture,

31
00:02:36,549 --> 00:02:40,664
or it could be one machine
to another machine.

32
00:02:40,664 --> 00:02:45,740
And this is commonly used in VPNs,
or virtual private networks.

33
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:53,631
So as an overview to set
up the IPsec tunnel,

34
00:02:53,631 --> 00:02:58,501
a protocol is a camp or IKE is used to set

35
00:02:58,501 --> 00:03:03,418
up the parameters and the keys used.

36
00:03:03,418 --> 00:03:07,274
And then you can authenticate each side.

37
00:03:07,274 --> 00:03:11,168
And then after all that,
the traffic can be exchanged.

38
00:03:11,168 --> 00:03:17,020
So we'll go through kind of
each of these steps in order.

39
00:03:17,020 --> 00:03:22,228
So the first is the SA-1
parameter exchange.

40
00:03:22,228 --> 00:03:25,317
So this is where the initiator, so

41
00:03:25,317 --> 00:03:31,079
this is the one who's initiating
the IPsec tunnel to the other.

42
00:03:31,079 --> 00:03:37,103
Says what algorithms it supports
across four main categories,

43
00:03:37,103 --> 00:03:41,453
what type of encryption
it can support using,

44
00:03:41,453 --> 00:03:45,707
what type of hashing it
could support using.

45
00:03:45,707 --> 00:03:51,859
We're going to get into
the Diffie-Hellman group, but

46
00:03:51,859 --> 00:03:56,985
these groups can impact
the level of security,

47
00:03:56,985 --> 00:04:03,924
but also the amount of CPU resources
that it takes to implement.

48
00:04:03,924 --> 00:04:08,908
And then how authentication
is going to be done.

49
00:04:08,908 --> 00:04:13,544
And so, this is based on the software or

50
00:04:13,544 --> 00:04:19,313
hardware that this
initiator has implemented.

51
00:04:19,313 --> 00:04:24,973
And then, the responder then
chooses among this list and says,

52
00:04:24,973 --> 00:04:29,674
I support AES, and that's a better,
I prefer that one.

53
00:04:29,674 --> 00:04:33,590
And I support this hash,
and I choose this.

54
00:04:33,590 --> 00:04:37,051
So whatever the responder it chooses and

55
00:04:37,051 --> 00:04:42,660
then sends back the results,
its choices back to the initiator.

56
00:04:44,360 --> 00:04:49,504
They then move into the next phase,
which is this Diffie-Hellman key exchange.

57
00:04:49,504 --> 00:04:53,792
And so, IPsec uses shared keys for
encryption.

58
00:04:53,792 --> 00:04:59,742
So it uses symmetric encryption and
uses that for integrity as well.

59
00:04:59,742 --> 00:05:04,297
And so what Diffie-Hellman is,
is a protocol to secretly create and

60
00:05:04,297 --> 00:05:06,480
share private keys.

61
00:05:06,480 --> 00:05:11,912
So if you recall, we saw this picture
where we can use a public key or

62
00:05:11,912 --> 00:05:16,277
asymmetric encryption to
exchange a session key, or

63
00:05:16,277 --> 00:05:22,500
something that could be shared so
they can use symmetric encryption.

64
00:05:22,500 --> 00:05:29,140
And so we saw this before, and we saw
that in the context of the RSA algorithm.

65
00:05:29,140 --> 00:05:32,608
So Diffie-Hellman is in that vein, but

66
00:05:32,608 --> 00:05:38,014
it allows two parties that have
no prior knowledge of each other

67
00:05:38,014 --> 00:05:43,830
to jointly establish a shared secret
key over an insecure channel.

68
00:05:44,940 --> 00:05:51,740
So this is quite useful, and
let's walk through how it works.

69
00:05:51,740 --> 00:05:56,563
So one side, we'll say it's the initiator,

70
00:05:56,563 --> 00:06:03,200
in this side picks two numbers,
a prime p and some base g.

71
00:06:03,200 --> 00:06:07,984
It sends those in clear text
to the other side that it's

72
00:06:07,984 --> 00:06:13,200
initiating a connection to,
we'll say, the responder.

73
00:06:15,140 --> 00:06:19,380
Then it will pick a secret x.

74
00:06:19,380 --> 00:06:25,935
And with this secret x that it's not
going to share, it will calculate g,

75
00:06:25,935 --> 00:06:31,960
which was this base that it
previously shared and picked.

76
00:06:31,960 --> 00:06:39,050
It'll calculate g to the x mod p,
and we'll call this h of A.

77
00:06:40,220 --> 00:06:40,782
All right?

78
00:06:40,782 --> 00:06:46,440
So it takes this h and
sends it to the other party.

79
00:06:48,720 --> 00:06:53,100
The other party, in this case, party B,

80
00:06:53,100 --> 00:06:57,741
or the responder will
pick its own secret y.

81
00:06:57,741 --> 00:07:01,934
And it's not going to share y, but

82
00:07:01,934 --> 00:07:10,020
it will share a calculation using
y hB equals g to the y mod p.

83
00:07:10,020 --> 00:07:13,865
All right?
And then it will send that calculated

84
00:07:13,865 --> 00:07:18,760
value h of B back to party A,
or the initiator.

85
00:07:18,760 --> 00:07:20,576
All right, so with this,

86
00:07:20,576 --> 00:07:25,214
they now actually have enough
information to have a shared key.

87
00:07:25,214 --> 00:07:27,260
So let's see what this is.

88
00:07:27,260 --> 00:07:35,952
So the shared key is actually if they
take the value that was shared with them,

89
00:07:35,952 --> 00:07:42,706
raised to the secret that they picked,
and then mod it by p.

90
00:07:42,706 --> 00:07:48,511
So this K equals h of B, so
party A is over on the left hand side,

91
00:07:48,511 --> 00:07:51,384
it received from B, the hB.

92
00:07:51,384 --> 00:07:56,263
It raises that to x, which is that secret

93
00:07:56,263 --> 00:08:00,982
that only A knows about and does mod p.

94
00:08:00,982 --> 00:08:06,420
Likewise, B or
the responder does the same calculation

95
00:08:06,420 --> 00:08:10,931
using the value hA,
which is what A sent to it,

96
00:08:10,931 --> 00:08:16,300
raises it to y, its secret,
and then does mod p.

97
00:08:16,300 --> 00:08:20,728
So why this works,
why this is the same is,

98
00:08:20,728 --> 00:08:27,122
if we actually expand out what h of B is,
it's g to the y mod p.

99
00:08:27,122 --> 00:08:31,994
So, A doesn't know what y is, but
it knows what's in parentheses,

100
00:08:31,994 --> 00:08:36,880
but we're expanding that out,
and then raises that to x.

101
00:08:36,880 --> 00:08:39,888
When you use the properties of mod,

102
00:08:39,888 --> 00:08:45,460
this actually just is the equivalent
to g to the xy mod p.

103
00:08:45,460 --> 00:08:48,500
So let's look at that on the other side.

104
00:08:48,500 --> 00:08:56,138
We take the hA, which is g to the x mod p,
we raise that to y.

105
00:08:56,138 --> 00:09:01,400
So even though B doesn't know what x is,
it knows what's in this parentheses.

106
00:09:01,400 --> 00:09:06,397
But again, if we did the math or
do the properties of mod,

107
00:09:06,397 --> 00:09:11,709
we can do when you raise something
that's mod to some power,

108
00:09:11,709 --> 00:09:15,102
you kind of distribute that through.

109
00:09:15,102 --> 00:09:18,220
So it would be g to the xy mod p.

110
00:09:18,220 --> 00:09:22,464
So, they both end up with the exact
same value, even though party

111
00:09:22,464 --> 00:09:26,978
A didn't know the value of y and
party B didn't know the value of x.

112
00:09:26,978 --> 00:09:31,900
And now why this is secure,
given what was exchanged p,

113
00:09:31,900 --> 00:09:35,250
g, hA and hB, the assumption here and

114
00:09:35,250 --> 00:09:40,594
others have calculated the math and
the difficulty of this,

115
00:09:40,594 --> 00:09:47,400
that it's hard to actually calculate
K with only this information.

116
00:09:47,400 --> 00:09:51,664
You need one of these secrets and
that's hard to guess.

117
00:09:54,779 --> 00:09:58,884
All right, so with that,
we now have a shared secret,

118
00:09:58,884 --> 00:10:03,366
a session key that could be used for
symmetric encryption.

119
00:10:03,366 --> 00:10:08,470
So the next part of IPsec is to
authenticate and this allows each party

120
00:10:08,470 --> 00:10:13,950
to verify that they're communicating
with who they think they are.

121
00:10:13,950 --> 00:10:18,052
Now, one of the parameters that
we saw in the parameter exchange,

122
00:10:18,052 --> 00:10:23,090
SA-1 parameter exchange, was how
they're going to do authentication.

123
00:10:23,090 --> 00:10:28,292
And there's two steps here or
two options listed here.

124
00:10:28,292 --> 00:10:33,629
One, is they could have had
a shared private key in advance or

125
00:10:33,629 --> 00:10:37,518
they could go with a digital certificate.

126
00:10:37,518 --> 00:10:44,101
So if you recall, this digital certificate
was some binding between an identity and

127
00:10:44,101 --> 00:10:48,834
a public key and
it's signed by a certificate authority.

128
00:10:48,834 --> 00:10:53,590
So that could be kind of how they
go through the authentication.

129
00:10:54,730 --> 00:11:00,365
And this step is encrypted using
the key that was decided in this

130
00:11:00,365 --> 00:11:07,490
kind of first part, the Diffie-Hellman
session keys that were calculated and

131
00:11:07,490 --> 00:11:14,430
used in the parameters that were
established in SA-1 parameter exchange.

132
00:11:16,610 --> 00:11:21,774
Then, they go into a second
SA-2 parameter exchange,

133
00:11:21,774 --> 00:11:26,030
where SA stands for
a security association.

134
00:11:26,030 --> 00:11:30,989
And that's basically just saying,
we're going to set up this encryption,

135
00:11:30,989 --> 00:11:36,810
we're agreeing on the parameters,
here's everything associated with it.

136
00:11:36,810 --> 00:11:41,969
And so they do this just in case
different parameters are desired for

137
00:11:41,969 --> 00:11:47,530
the encrypted traffic, then for
the authentication step.

138
00:11:47,530 --> 00:11:54,214
And this step can also be used to set
up what's known as traffic selectors.

139
00:11:54,214 --> 00:11:58,406
And this determines what traffic
is allowed through the tunnel.

140
00:11:58,406 --> 00:12:02,740
So this is a five tuple of
the IP source and destination,

141
00:12:02,740 --> 00:12:08,750
the protocol like TCP or UDP and the
transport source and destination ports.

142
00:12:10,690 --> 00:12:13,720
And then finally,
we can have the traffic exchange.

143
00:12:13,720 --> 00:12:18,653
So after all of that, we have keys
calculated, we know how we're going to

144
00:12:18,653 --> 00:12:23,934
encrypt it, but there's different
modes of how traffic can be exchanged.

145
00:12:23,934 --> 00:12:27,456
So there's effectively
two different categories.

146
00:12:27,456 --> 00:12:33,687
The first is whether it's just
the header in the HAH mode for

147
00:12:33,687 --> 00:12:40,168
authenticating header, and
this just provides integrity,

148
00:12:40,168 --> 00:12:46,151
or we can have ESP for
encapsulating security payload and

149
00:12:46,151 --> 00:12:51,283
this provides integrity and
confidentiality.

150
00:12:51,283 --> 00:12:55,730
And then we have two other modes.

151
00:12:55,730 --> 00:12:59,099
So we would choose one of these, Or ESP,

152
00:12:59,099 --> 00:13:02,670
and then one of these,
transport or tunnel.

153
00:13:04,550 --> 00:13:08,466
And transport is mostly used for
single endpoints,

154
00:13:08,466 --> 00:13:12,630
whereas tunnels are mostly used for
whole sites.

155
00:13:12,630 --> 00:13:18,250
And so we could see the illustration
of tunnel versus transport.

156
00:13:18,250 --> 00:13:23,794
So in transport mode, so this is for
like a single kind of endpoint.

157
00:13:23,794 --> 00:13:26,764
So there, we're not encrypting or

158
00:13:26,764 --> 00:13:31,238
hiding in any manner the IP
header of the endpoints.

159
00:13:31,238 --> 00:13:35,645
We're just encrypting and
authenticating or

160
00:13:35,645 --> 00:13:41,570
doing integrity checks of all
the data in this IP packet.

161
00:13:41,570 --> 00:13:46,744
Tunnel mode, here would be
the actual IP address source and

162
00:13:46,744 --> 00:13:49,802
destination of the end machines.

163
00:13:49,802 --> 00:13:55,370
But we're tunneling it between kind of
those two machines that are doing for

164
00:13:55,370 --> 00:13:56,712
the whole site.

165
00:13:56,712 --> 00:14:00,150
So we put on this new IP header.

166
00:14:00,150 --> 00:14:04,220
So this would be the IP
header of the source and

167
00:14:04,220 --> 00:14:09,554
destination of those kind of
the edge point of the sites.

168
00:14:09,554 --> 00:14:15,932
And then we can encrypt the rest of all
of the packet, including the IP header.

169
00:14:18,364 --> 00:14:23,664
Now there's a lot of software
that's available for IPsec.

170
00:14:23,664 --> 00:14:29,094
All of the major network vendors
have routers or firewalls.

171
00:14:29,094 --> 00:14:33,010
So Juniper, Cisco, Palo Alto and so forth.

172
00:14:33,010 --> 00:14:37,650
The cloud providers like AWS, Azure, GCP.

173
00:14:37,650 --> 00:14:41,610
They have VPN services
which are just IPsec.

174
00:14:41,610 --> 00:14:45,310
And then there's open source software,
a popular one and

175
00:14:45,310 --> 00:14:47,890
a common one to use would be Strong Swan.

176
00:14:49,590 --> 00:14:52,138
So just to summarize with IPsec,

177
00:14:52,138 --> 00:14:57,599
it leveraged many of the techniques
we discussed in the basics lesson.

178
00:14:57,599 --> 00:15:01,657
It talked about message
integrity through HMAC.

179
00:15:01,657 --> 00:15:04,286
It uses confidentiality or

180
00:15:04,286 --> 00:15:09,669
provides confidentiality
using a shared private key.

181
00:15:09,669 --> 00:15:14,701
Exchanged shared keys
using Diffie Hellman, and

182
00:15:14,701 --> 00:15:19,733
then provided authentication
with public keys or

183
00:15:19,733 --> 00:15:22,617
pre-shared private keys.

184
00:15:22,617 --> 00:15:25,046
So that covers IPsec.

185
00:15:25,046 --> 00:15:30,510
And next,
we're going to talk now about RPKI.

186
00:15:30,510 --> 00:15:31,021
All right, thanks.